# Mobile — showing progress without blocking the UI

React Native runs touch handling, layout, and every JS callback on one thread. So any work a
high-frequency event does — a download's byte callback, a playback position tick, an upload, a sync
counter — is work a tap has to wait behind. The device that reveals this is a five-year-old Android
phone, and the symptom is not a slow spinner: it is the **second** button press appearing to do
nothing.

Two rules cover it. Both are about where state lives and who is told about it.

## A tap changes state in memory, before any await

The control the user pressed must change on that press. Not after SQLite, not after a native-cache
projection, not after the network.

```typescript
// BAD — three awaits before the icon changes, so a second tap lands on a stale control
const existing = await repository.getByItemIdText(item.id_text);
await repository.upsert(record); // also rebuilds and writes the native cache
notify();

// GOOD — memory first, durable write after
const existing = store.get(item.id_text); // already hydrated
store.put(record); // subscribers paint this frame
void repository.upsert(record).catch(logInDev);
```

Keep the in-memory mirror hydrated once per session (every hook that renders the affordance can
ask), hold records **immutable**, and replace only the one that changed. That last part is what lets
a row's `setState` receive the same reference and skip its re-render, so one item's transition does
not repaint the other thirty-nine.

SQLite stays the durable store. It is not the thing the UI reads while something is happening.

## Split the notification channel by frequency

A single "something changed" callback forces every subscriber to pay the cost of the noisiest
producer. Give coarse and fine events separate channels, and make the fine one **opt-in**.

- **Coarse** (status, set membership, completion): notify on the leading edge so a tap paints
  immediately, then collapse a burst into one trailing pass (~100 ms).
- **Fine** (bytes, position, counters): trailing edge only, at a rate a person can actually read —
  **2 Hz is plenty**. Nobody perceives a percentage moving faster, and it is roughly two orders of
  magnitude less work than repainting per event.

`downloads/downloadStore.ts` is the worked example: `subscribe` for status, `subscribeToProgress`
for bytes, `batch` for a bulk operation that would otherwise fire once per row.

## Never do these on a high-frequency tick

Each one was a real cause of a frozen download button:

- A **SQLite write or read**. Persist the fine value on a slow heartbeat (a few seconds) plus the
  moments it is actually read — pause, failure, completion.
- A **native-cache projection** (CarPlay / Android Auto). Rebuild it when the set it describes
  changes, never when a counter moves. See **mobile-carplay-android-auto**.
- A **filesystem walk** or storage measurement.
- A **full-table read** to recompute a badge or a count.
- **Waking every subscriber**, which multiplies all of the above by the number of mounted screens.

## Put progress detail where the user asked for it

This is the UX half, and it removes most of the cost rather than optimizing it.

| Surface | What it shows | Subscribes to progress? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| List row (can be 40 on screen) | Busy spinner, no number | **No** — status only |
| Detail screen for one item | Percentage or bar | Yes |
| The screen dedicated to the activity (My Library → Downloads) | Per-item bar | Yes |
| Global activity bar, tab badges | Message + spinner (downloads), a count (tabs) | **No** — derived from statuses |

A row that says *something is happening here* has already told the user what a row can usefully
tell them; the number belongs on the screen they open to see the number. Determinate bars also lie
more often than they inform — a transfer with an unknown content length has no honest ratio, so
prefer indeterminate until a total is known.

**Accessibility:** a busy row still needs `accessibilityState={{ busy: true }}` and a label that
says what pressing it does. `accessibilityValue` with a live percentage is fine on the single-item
surfaces above, and is exactly what to leave off a list row — a screen reader announcing a changing
number on forty rows is as unusable as it is expensive. See **screen-reader-accessibility**.

